  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 AM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nLittle change to the general pattern and the sensible weather is \nexpected today from the last few days. We remain between a ridge off \nthe NC Outer Banks and a trough in the vicinity of the MS River. \nThis trough will make slow eastward progress, and may contribute to \nsome high rain chances in a large portion of the area on Saturday. \n\nToday is expected to be another day of convection developing in the \nterrain of the mountains and Cumberland Plateau, with slow steering \nflow. Storm propagation will be mainly due to outflow from dying \ncells. Localized flooding under these slow-moving cells with heavy \nrainfall will again be the main threat this afternoon. MLCAPE around \n1000 L/kg with little to no shear should mean a very low threat of \nstrong to severe storms. Saturday will have better midlevel forcing \nwith the approaching shortwave trough. As a result, convection may \ninitiate earlier in the day, and not only in the higher terrain. \nLocalized flooding will be the main threat again, with a low risk of \ndamaging downburst winds. \n\nBy Sunday, upper-level flow shifts to the NW as the trough moves \neast of our area. Overall, there is a signal for less moisture, \nwhich would limit coverage compared to previous days. Drying of the \ncolumn continues into Monday as a high pressure ridge will be \nbuilding to our west. By Tuesday and Wednesday, a broad upper trough \nbegins to develop across the eastern Conus, and embedded shortwaves \nwill being to bring higher rain chances to the area. The combination \nof low-level heating and cooling aloft due to shortwaves will \nproduce a more notable convective environment. Current indications \nsuggest lapse rates reaching 6 C/km to even 7 C/km, which would \nprovide greater instability. A lot is uncertain about the exact \nplacement of this environment, but the potential for strong to \nsevere storms is something to keep an eye on for next week.",
